From: Martin Ellis <malard@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:03:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [settings] Don't hold a setting's lock while its change
 callbacks run (#29011)

* fixed: don't hold a setting's lock while its change callbacks run

CSetting::SetValue held the setting's exclusive lock (m_critical) across
OnSettingChanging and OnSettingChanged. A callback that causes the same
setting to be read from another thread then deadlocks: the reader takes a
shared lock on m_critical, which blocks on the exclusive lock the writer
still holds, while the writer blocks waiting for the reader.

That is xbmc/xbmc#20371. Setting videoscreen.blankdisplays over JSON-RPC runs
on a web-server thread: CSettingBool::SetValue holds the lock and calls
CDisplaySettings::OnSettingChanging, which calls
CGraphicContext::SetVideoResolution, which posts a synchronous message to the
main thread and waits. The main thread then runs SetVideoResolutionInternal,
which reads the same setting back via GetBool - and blocks on the lock the web
thread is holding. Kodi freezes. It only happens off the GUI thread (a GUI
change is already on the main thread, so no cross-thread hop) and where
SetVideoResolutionInternal reads the setting, i.e. Windows/macOS in fullscreen.

Invoking callbacks while holding a lock they might re-enter is the underlying
hazard, so the lock is now held only for the value mutation and the revert,
and released before the callbacks. A value read during a callback sees the
pending value, which is the correct trade-off for not calling out under a lock.

The sequence is the same for every setting type, so it lives once in
CSetting::ApplyValue rather than in each SetValue: take the lock, reject an
unchanged or invalid value, store, run the callbacks unlocked, and revert if
they refuse. The bool, int, number and string settings are written through it.
CSettingList keeps its own copy, because it rejects on size and element type as
well as validity and compares its elements by their string form rather than by
==.

Releasing the lock opens a window in which another thread's SetValue can be
accepted before this call's callbacks reject it. The revert therefore restores
the old value only if the setting still holds the value this call wrote. That
test cannot distinguish such a value from the same value written again by
another thread, so the revert is best effort rather than exact - which is still
strictly better than the unconditional overwrite it replaces.

TestSettingsManager.ValueIsReadableFromAnotherThreadDuringOnSettingChanging
pins the deadlock: an OnSettingChanging handler reads the setting from another
thread and waits for that read. TestSettingsManager.
RejectedChangeDoesNotUndoAConcurrentWrite pins the revert: a handler writes a
second value from another thread and then rejects the first, and the second
value must survive. Both waits are bounded, so a regression fails the test
rather than hanging it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

* [settings] Apply a setting's writes one at a time, and join the threads its tests start

Callbacks still run with the setting's lock released, so another thread can
read it meanwhile; a new per-setting writer lock is held across the store
and the callbacks instead. A second write waits for the first rather than
seeing its tentative value: before, writing the value a pending change had
stored returned success at once, and the pending change's rejection then
reverted it. The four functions that call SetValue holding the setting's
lock take the writer lock first, so the two are always taken in the same
order.

With writes serialised a rejection restores the old value outright. The
test that wrote the setting from another thread inside its callback is
replaced by one whose threads are joined, so a regression fails the test
instead of reaching a destroyed callback.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

